6 REPLACES :action with the strategic selector's pick, which is handed only :scheduler-habit-ranking and a trace id. All 20 S2 records carry :selection-boundary :reason-bearing-strategic-policy and :habit-prior-applied? false. So S3 as written would change the recorded :tau and :softmax-weights and not the action. WHAT I DID NOT ESTABLISH, and it must not be read as established: whether anything downstream of the decision map consumes :tau or :softmax-weights. I did not audit that; RUN8 must, rather than treating this as a claim that nothing does. (4) WHAT I CHECKED, so the review is auditable: re-ran run7_beta_arms.clj against the S2 trace at f625754 -- output byte-identical to the committed ARMS.txt, all 308 lines, and its own src-vs-local solver control reads delta 0.0 (ARMS.txt:2); measured ln E directly off the trace on ticks 1, 10 and 20 of 20 usable fields rather than taking RUN7's word for it; read :selection-boundary, :habit-prior-applied?, :tau, :tau-spread and :beta-source off all 20 records; read the four code sites above. Registry edit recorded as C23. p4ng negative_controls PASS (16 negative, 10 positive) and pointer_check 226 pointers 0 unresolved before the commit. RUN7 remains :done-unreviewed and nothing here reviews it."
   :slice-b2a "DONE, reviewed by claude-20 2026-09-01. futon2 4625524 (codex-21) + cf5c7de (pin repair) + f4c8f9e (control broadened). converge-beta takes :log-priors and :log-prior-placement of :none | :pi | :both, DEFAULT :none, so a reader that did not ask for ln E does not move. :pi is friston2017.txt:684 as printed; :both is spm_MDP_VB_X.m:964, the implementation form. This is the :choices :pi-zero-form choice point becoming RUNNABLE rather than being decided; no ruling is written anywhere by this slice. Pure: carry-beta, war_machine.clj and trace.clj are untouched, so the FUTON_WM_BETA_DARK path is unchanged and nothing consumes the new option. THE TEST I MOST WANTED IS THERE AND I DID NOT HAVE TO ASK FOR IT: three-log-prior-arms-are-distinct-on-field-shaped-input-test asserts three distinct betas from the three placements. The arms are measured where they DIFFER, not at a point where they coincide -- the AGENTS.md rule, applied by the author unprompted. Uniform-lnE-is-a-no-op and shift-invariance are also pinned, so the no-op cases are proven rather than assumed. Five explicit validation errors, including :log-priors supplied with :none placement. clj-kondo 0 errors 0 warnings, check-parens OK, and the tests re-run at HEAD by me: policy-precision 25 tests / 138 assertions, war-machine + trace 75 tests / 294 assertions, 0 failures 0 errors; negative_controls PASS (16 negative, 10 positive); pointer_check 218 pointers, 0 unresolved. THE STRONGEST CHECK IS ONE NO DELIVERED TEST COULD MAKE, because it needs the run artifacts: src two new arms REPRODUCE RUN7 PUBLISHED GAMMAS EXACTLY, over the 20 carried ticks of the real S2 field, against a solver that is not the one under test -- run7_beta_arms.clj carries its own bisection, written before this change and untouched by it. Carried from beta_0 = 1.0 over runs/2026-09-01-s2/wm-trace-s2.edn: :none 0.9667979197554702, :pi 0.9040600731260442, :both 0.9712374387398894, each delta 0.00e+00 against runs/2026-09-01-s2/ARMS.txt:169,216,263, 20/20 converged and bracketed in every arm. So this is the same object the arms measured, not a second implementation agreeing to four decimals -- and the :none arm reproducing the run own recorded betas is a second, on-real-data proof that the default path did not move. The arms differ by about 7% in gamma on real field data. THREE REVIEW FINDINGS, all fixed rather than re-belled. (1) cf5c7de: the default-path control anchored to e9bcf35, P-organise-the-library from an UNRELATED LANE -- whatever happened to be HEAD when the work started. Its content for this file is byte-identical to RUN7 commit, so the control gave the right answer, but the anchor names nothing a reader can interpret. That is the moving-anchor finding from the RUN5 review returning as HEAD-at-dispatch instead of HEAD~, in a repo where 039b0b8 had just pinned the sibling controls to 183749a for exactly this reason. Repinned to 039b0b8, the commit whose default path this control claims is unmoved, with the reason in the def. (2) f4c8f9e: that identity control ran on ONE input, so it would have passed while any other branch drifted. Broadened to five classes -- both solvers, a bound cut short, an unbracketed root, a two-candidate field -- with an assertion that the five really are distinct states (some converged, some not, one hit-bound, one unbracketed), so the class list cannot rot into five runs of the same thing. (3) f4c8f9e: the cross-solver agreement test covered :pi only; :both is the SPM form and the likelier candidate for the live wiring, so it now runs under both placements. PACKET CORRECTION for future dispatches: this repo :test alias uses :exec-fn, so clojure -M:test -n <ns> treats -n as a filename. The working form is clojure -X:test :nses [<ns>]. My packet specified the wrong one, from the AGENTS.md test section that is written for futon3c, and codex-21 caught it. A COLLISION ON THIS ROW, worth recording because it is the shared-repository hazard arriving in the ledger rather than in the code: TWO claude-20 work seats did slice (b2a) concurrently and both wrote an :slice-b2a to this row within one minute (afa3d02 at 19:32:23, and a second insert that made worklist_check fail with Duplicate key :slice-b2a). The ledger check caught it, which is what it is for; this entry is the reconciled union of the two, and both seats reviews are in it. The parallel-lane rule applies to the ledger too -- one row, one writer at a time. WHAT (b2) STILL OWES: the live wiring itself -- FUTON_WM_TAU_MODE :variational-beta-gamma at policy.clj:72-80 with the parser at war_machine.clj:238-248 -- is staged as RUN8 and is BLOCKED on RUN7 being read, so it was deliberately NOT done here and must not be taken ahead of that gate. Also still owed by (b2): naming which placement the live path takes (RUN7 arms are the evidence), and naming the fallback for a tick whose gamma could not be solved. carry-beta pre-empted the second in code -- only converged? AND bracketed? carries, otherwise :held-unsolved -- and RUN7 reviewer-should-check (5) asks whether that order was right; (b2) settles it in the row."
   :slice-b1 "DONE and reviewed 2026-09-01. futon2 a75f763 (codex-21) + 2598fbe (owner review fixes). src/futon2/aif/policy_precision.clj iterates Appendix B discrete relaxation beta_next = beta + step.gamma^2.[(beta_prior - beta) + (pi - pi_0).G] to eq. 2.7 fixed point; pure, wired into nothing. 8 tests / 26 assertions, clj-kondo clean, check-parens OK. F_pi enters UNSCALED, gamma multiplying G alone, per eq. 2.7. beta_prior required, no default. converged?/hit-bound?/iterations returned as data. THE HEADLINE IS NOT WHAT WAS REPORTED. codex-21 table said beta_0 = 2.0 and 5.0 DID NOT CONVERGE at the 256-iteration bound. Recomputed on the same field: ALL FOUR CONVERGE. beta_0 = 2.0 converges at 258 -- a bound two iterations short -- with gamma identical to eight decimals (delta 7.8e-11); beta_0 = 5.0 converges at 1726. Reporting a bound artifact as a property of the problem is the same class as the tau = 1 comparison earlier today: a measurement made where it could not have given the other answer. WHAT THE FIELD ACTUALLY SHOWS, and it is an operational consequence of Joe carried ruling: ITERATIONS GROW STEEPLY WITH BETA -- 5, 24, 258, 1726 for beta_0 = 0.5, 1, 2, 5 -- because the effective step is step.gamma^2 = step/beta^2, so the rate per iteration falls off like 1/beta^2. Under J4 beta IS the previous tick posterior, so a beta that drifts upward makes each tick quadratically slower to solve. Default :max-iterations raised 256 -> 4096, covering the measured 1726 with headroom, with the measurement in the docstring so a caller sets the bound knowingly. SECOND FINDING: :step-size 0.25 is not merely the paper simulation value, it is inside a STABILITY BOUNDARY THAT MOVES WITH BETA. At step 1.0, beta_0 = 0.5 and 1.0 fail to converge in 200,000 iterations and settle on the WRONG gamma (0.319 against the true 2.009); at step 4.0 they diverge. Meanwhile beta_0 = 2 and 5 converge FASTER at the larger steps (57 and 425 at step 1.0). So the usable step depends on the carried beta. Recorded in the docstring with the numbers. THIRD FINDING, fixed: THE GUARD WAS ONE-SIDED. beta -> 0 was floored, because gamma = 1/beta goes infinite. beta -> infinity was unguarded, and gives gamma -> 0 with a near-uniform pi -- a degenerate answer that LOOKS valid. Observed at step 4.0: beta reached 2e12 and gamma 5e-13 with no exception, since those values are finite. :converged? was false so it was not silent, but a caller ignoring the flag gets a plausible number. Added :beta-ceiling (default 1e6) with :beta-ceiling-hit? and a count, symmetric with the floor, and a test that DRIVES the divergence rather than asserting the guard exists. WHAT THE REFERENCE DOES, from V5/C463: SPM pays NEITHER cost. Its step is qb = qb - dFdg/2, flat, with no gamma^2 (spm_MDP_VB_X.m:973) -- so the 1/beta^2 slowdown belongs to the appendix form beta-dot = gamma^2 . eps_gamma that our first implementation followed, not to the paper code. And it does not converge: Ni = 16 is a fixed count with no break (:244, :959-984), so SPM gamma on a hard field is the sixteenth iterate, not the root. That is what makes bisection ours-alone for a reason rather than by accident. SOLVER REPLACED AFTER claude-1 READ THE ABOVE, and it dissolves the first two findings rather than managing them (futon2 0fb8b27). Their point: eq. 2.7 defines a SCALAR FIXED POINT IN ONE UNKNOWN, and iterating to it is the paper scheme, not a property the object has. Both the 1/beta^2 slowdown and the moving stability boundary follow from stepping in beta with a fixed step, since the effective step is step/beta^2. Checked on the real field before changing anything: the residual beta_prior + (pi(beta) - pi_0(beta)).G - beta is STRICTLY DECREASING with EXACTLY ONE SIGN CHANGE over exp(-6)..exp(6), for every beta_prior in {0.5, 1, 2, 5}. So the root is unique there and bisection reaches it. Measured evaluations, bisect against gradient: 70/5, 70/24, 70/258, 68/1726, 65/171018 at beta_prior 50, and 61 against >200000 at beta_prior 500 where the gradient solver never converges and is wrong in the eighth decimal. The two agree to 1e-9 or better wherever the gradient one converges. BISECTION IS NOW THE DEFAULT; :solver :gradient is retained as the paper stated scheme and as a cross-check. Cost is FLAT IN BETA and slightly cheaper for large beta, so a drifting carried beta does not make later ticks more expensive -- the cost I recorded above as a consequence of J4 was a consequence of one solver. There is no step size, so no stability boundary; the :max-iterations 4096 default I raised is no longer load-bearing for the default path. :beta-floor and :beta-ceiling become the BRACKET rather than clamps, so the guard work is not wasted, and the bracket ends are checked for opposite signs -- :bracketed? false with both end residuals is returned rather than halving to a meaningless midpoint. Monotonicity is a numerical observation on one field, NOT a proof, and the docstring and the bracket check both say so. THIRD CONSTRAINT ON (b2), decided rather than escalated: PI_0 INCLUDES ln E. SPM computes pu = spm_softmax(qE + w*Q) at spm_MDP_VB_X.m:964, carrying the habit prior in pi_0 as well as in pi; friston2017.txt:684 gives pi_0 = sigma(-gamma.G) with no E; our converge-beta today puts it in neither. Adopting the code form, with claude-1 grounds: with E in BOTH, (pi - pi_0) isolates what F_pi alone does to the posterior, which is exactly what the beta update is meant to measure. The paper text form is recorded as the alternative. This is NOT a J row -- the choice does not bite on the live path today, since the habit prior is not applied to selection (C3), and it can be revisited with evidence once ln E is live rather than ruled on now without any. TWO CONSTRAINTS ON (b2), from claude-1 reading the solver change. (a) THE ONE SURVIVING SOLVER PARAMETER IS THE TOLERANCE, and its units are now stated: convergence is |residual| <= tolerance with the residual in BETA units, the bracket-width test being a backstop against a non-terminating loop only, and :converged? computed from the residual at the returned beta either way. Beta units are the right units and the docstring now says why -- gamma = 1/beta and the softmax consumes -G/tau, so tau = 1/gamma = BETA, verified numerically (beta_posterior 0.9989859197 against 1/gamma 0.9989859197). The tolerance is therefore a tolerance on the temperature the machine actually uses; a tolerance stated in gamma would be the awkward one. (b) A TICK WHERE GAMMA COULD NOT BE SOLVED IS A DATUM, NOT A SILENT FALLBACK. effective-temperature must treat :bracketed? false exactly as it treats :converged? false: the run record carries it, and whatever the fallback is -- hold the previous beta, or fall back to the prior -- is NAMED in the (b2) row so a later reader of a run record can tell solved from held. Not yet decided; (b2) decides it and records it. FOURTH, minor and correct as delivered: the 07-04 record carries :G-total but no persisted prediction fields, so codex-21 derived predictions from the record actions and first-record state with the pure forward model and scored the second observation with {:absent-variance :floor}. Same route the I2(b1) review used, and the right one for a pre-I3 record; noted so nobody later reads these numbers as having come from persisted predictions."
   :statement "Implement policy precision as gamma = 1/beta with the beta update of friston2017 eq. 2.7 (beta <- beta + (pi - pi0) . G), replacing effective-temperature's selection-gain proxy on the live path, behind a flag until compared."
   :acceptance "policy.clj computes tau = 1/gamma from the beta update; a run record shows gamma changing tick to tick from G and pi; the selection-gain path remains available under FUTON_WM_TAU_MODE for comparison; tests; clj-kondo clean; registry :temperature-update :observed updated by a reviewed C row; sha here."}
  {:id :I2 :class :I :status :open :loop-mode :one-slice-per-invocation :owner :claude-20 :assigned "2026-09-01 by Joe: claude-20 orchestrates under the coding-handoff protocol (Codex implements in small handoffs; claude-20 reviews); registry/ledger rows it produces are read by claude-1"
   :slice-b1 "DONE and reviewed 2026-09-01. futon2 764db75 (codex-21) + cb9a7f4 (owner review fix). src/futon2/aif/policy_free_energy.clj computes the horizon-one Gaussian F_pi per candidate; nothing consumes it yet. 8 tests / 19 assertions, clj-kondo clean, check-parens OK. REVIEW FINDING, fixed not re-belled: the delivered function could not be called on WM data at all. It treated every zero variance as a deterministic claim and rejected any mismatch -- but forward-model/predict gives a variance of 0.0 to every channel the action model does not touch, marked :variance-status {:status :absent :reason :deterministic-by-action-model}, which means NO PREDICTION, not a claim to predict exactly. Measured on wm-trace-2026-07-04: 14 channels, 12 absent per candidate, 7 moving between consecutive ticks, so scoring the tick-t prediction against the tick-(t+1) observation threw on :mathematics-pct at residual 0.001. The delivered tests all passed because every fixture was 1-2 synthetic channels; a WM-shaped fixture (2 predicted channels, 3 declared absent) reproduces the rejection, and is now committed. Fix: :absent-variance option, :reject still the default, :floor uses :variance-floor 0.01 -- the same floor futon2.aif.precision applies to its own variance -- for absent channels ONLY; a zero with no :absent status still rejects. No silent default turns a rejection into a number. MEASURED FOR SLICE (b2), so the wiring decision is not taken blind: over the real 110-candidate field of 07-04 scored against the next tick, F_pi takes 19 distinct values with a spread of 0.82 and a largest bucket of 35 candidates sharing one value; G over the same field takes 30 distinct values with a spread of 9.62. B.9 adds ln E - F_pi - G unscaled, so F_pi would move the posterior by roughly a twelfth of what G does. It is a real, discriminating, SMALL correction -- (b2) should report the rank changes it actually causes rather than claiming the term is now present."
   :slice-b2-followup "Three corrections to the b2 review, 2026-09-01, two of them claude-1 findings and one mine. (1) THE SCALING IS RESOLVED BY SOURCE, not by the invariance argument. friston2017 eq. 2.7 is pi = sigma(-F - gamma.G): gamma multiplies G ALONE and F enters unscaled (friston2017.txt:660-666). B.9 has no temperature at all, so eq. 2.7 is the one source that has one, and it puts tau nowhere near F. :unscaled is therefore settled and the :by-tau arm is CLOSED, not open. The tau-invariance property stands as the reason the formalism form is the sensible one, not as the reason for choosing it. (2) THE ARGMAX TEST DOES NOT COVER ENACTMENT, and my summary overclaimed by saying F_pi changes nothing the machine does. On the close-loop! path the enacted action is the first ranked candidate that PASSES ITS ACT GATE (V1/C460: in 50 of 50 comparable records, not the rank-1 selection), so a reshuffle of ranks 2..110 can change what is enacted with the argmax fixed. I ran that test: the first gate-passing candidate is M-bayesian-structure-learning with and without F_pi at every tau. BUT IT IS A VACUOUS TEST ON THIS FIELD -- 07-04 carries act-gate verdicts for 2 of 110 candidates and only one passes, so the first passer is invariant under ANY permutation, including one that reverses the field. Recorded as not-distinguishable, not as agreement. (3) MY OWN LIMIT, found while running (2): the persisted :rank does NOT equal ascending :G-total order on this trace (rank 1 is M-first-flights; min G-total is M-canon-fingerprint-store). July records carry :G-total but not :controller-score, which is what rank-actions actually sorted on. So both codex-22 measurement and my re-measurement order the field by a PROXY, not by the machine own ranking, and the 82-of-110 figure is a statement about that proxy. CORRECTED by claude-1, 2026-09-01, and the correction is sharper than what I wrote: the machine ORDERING is recorded -- :rank is the machine own rank, not a proxy -- and what is missing is the SCORE it was sorted on. rank-actions sorted on :controller-score, which July records do not carry; :G-total is a different quantity that happens to be present, agreeing with rank order at 2 of 110 positions. So the ordering is known and the effect of ADDING F_pi to it cannot be computed, because the base score F_pi would be added to is not in the record. Two consequences: H1b 50/50 is untouched, since it compares the recorded :rank and :decision against the realized policy and neither needs the missing score; and (b2) effect on selection is UNMEASURED on real data rather than measured as nil. This is precisely what I3 trace write exists to fix: a field recorded after it can answer the question at the machine own grain, and this one cannot."
   :slice-b2 "DONE and reviewed 2026-09-01. futon2 9b1c425 (codex-22). Flag :f-pi-policy-posterior?, default false; :f-pi-scaling :unscaled (B.9's form) or :by-tau. One seam only, softmax-weights, as its docstring requires. 34 tests / 134 assertions, clj-kondo clean, check-parens OK. Flag-off verified against policy.clj at 9b1c425^ loaded as a parallel namespace over five input classes: identical. THE ANSWER TO THE QUESTION THAT MATTERS: the ARGMAX DOES NOT CHANGE. On the 110-candidate field of 07-04 record 1 scored against record 2, at every tau I tried (0.1, 0.5, 1, 2, 5) and under both scalings, F_pi reshuffles between 20 and 106 of the 110 ranks -- and never changes which candidate is first. So F_pi is present, discriminating, and changes nothing the machine would do on the one field we can test. That is the J2 shape one level up: not computed-and-consumed-by-nothing, but consumed-with-no-effect-on-the-decision. It is a bound from ONE field, not a general claim; a second field could show otherwise, and until one does this term should not be described as changing selection. REVIEW FINDING: the delivered measurement reported unscaled and by-tau as separate results with identical numbers (82 moved, max 35). They were identical because it measured at the live tau, and the live tau is exactly 1.0 -- effective-temperature with the default selection gain 1.0 in :selection-gain-only mode -- where F/tau = F by construction. The comparison compared a thing with itself. Re-measured across tau: at 0.1 unscaled moves 20 ranks and by-tau 82; at 2.0 unscaled moves 106. The two are genuinely different objects. AND A PROPERTY WORTH THE ROW: under :by-tau with no habit prior the score is -(G + F_pi)/tau, a monotone transform of (G + F_pi), so the RANKING IS INVARIANT IN TAU -- 82 moved, max 35, at every tau tested. Under :unscaled tau trades G against F_pi and the ranking moves with it. So the scaling choice is not cosmetic: :by-tau makes F_pi influence exactly proportional to G and denies tau any power to balance them; :unscaled, which is what B.9 prints, does not. THE CHOICE IS CURRENTLY UNFALSIFIABLE ON LIVE DATA, which is the finding to carry: the live path holds tau at 1.0 because the selection gain never updates (C2, C454, fold-realized-outcome sim-only), and at tau = 1 the two scalings coincide exactly. Nothing on the live path can distinguish them until either the selection gain moves or J4 lands a gamma that does."
   :slice-c "DONE and reviewed 2026-09-01. futon2 4c53664 (codex-20) + review fixes. Flag FUTON_WM_FPI_DARK, default OFF. Each tick scores the PREVIOUS tick persisted predictions against THIS tick observation and persists the result; nothing consumes it. 63 tests / 249 assertions, clj-kondo clean, check-parens OK. git show --stat confirms no policy.clj and no policy_precision.clj change: selection is untouched. THE TRAP WAS AVOIDED, and it is worth recording that it was: rank/N is tick-local, so joining by rank would score one action prediction against another action outcome. codex-20 joined by SEMANTIC ACTION IDENTITY (habit-prior/policy-key, type + target), kept the previous tick rank/N only as the result KEY so results still join to the predictions that produced them, and said so in the docstring. AND IT SPOTTED A DEPENDENCY I HAD MISSED: f-pi-for-candidate {:absent-variance :floor} only floors channels whose :variance-status says :absent, and I3 did not persist :variance-status. Without it every real tick would have rejected on the first moved zero-variance channel and been recorded as an absence -- the I2(b1) failure returning through the trace. trace.clj now persists :prediction-variance-status inside the same flag. VERIFIED END TO END ON REAL DATA rather than on the fixture: built a previous-tick record through the real strip-ranked-action from the 07-04 field, scored it against the next record observation -- 20 of 20 matched, 20 of 20 scored, all :status :present with values in 61.63..61.69. Several candidates share a value exactly, which is the discrimination bucketing already recorded under (b1). ONE REVIEW FINDING, fixed: the whole-tick absence and the per-candidate results were BOTH bare maps, so a consumer calling vals on {:status :absent :reason :no-previous-trace-record} would have got (:absent :no-previous-trace-record) as though those were F_pi numbers -- the absence-as-success class, one level up. Both now share one envelope, {:status ... :by-candidate-id ...}, with a test that pins the distinction. Nothing consumes this yet, which is exactly when to fix a shape. Also documented that the two flags are COUPLED: FUTON_WM_FPI_DARK alone records :previous-trace-has-no-policy-predictions every tick -- correct, and useless."
   :slice-b2-constraint "Before (b2) is dispatched: B.9 prints pi = sigma(ln E - F_pi - G) with NO temperature -- the paper has no tau. The WM's live posterior divides G by tau (policy.clj:244-246). Whether F_pi is divided by the same tau or enters unscaled is therefore a choice B.9 does not make for us, and it changes the size of the term (measured at about a twelfth of G unscaled). The row (b2) produces must STATE which, or its rank-change count is not attributable to anything. Raised by claude-1, 2026-09-01."
   :statement "Implement the per-policy free energy F_pi and include it in the policy posterior pi = sigma(ln E - F_pi - G) (parr2022 B.9), then retire the Laplace channel F at R8 once F_pi is consumed."
