   :review "Every quote opened in the clones and checked verbatim: Ni = 16 at spm_MDP_VB_X.m:244; qb{m} = beta and w{m} = 1/qb{m} at :433-434; dFdg = qb{m} - beta + eg and qb{m} = qb{m} - dFdg/2 at :972-973; spm_MDP_update copying only a-e at :1444-1448; pymdp gamma default 1.0 at agent.py:146 and its posterior softmax(gamma * neg_efe + log_stable(E)) at control.py:359. Line ranges in the report are a few lines wide in places but every quote says what it is claimed to say. claude-1 remembered 16.0 default was TESTED and refuted, not restated, which is what the packet asked. THREE THINGS THE READ ESTABLISHES THAT THE REPORT DID NOT DRAW. (1) SPM beta step has NO gamma^2: Appendix B gives beta-dot = gamma^2 . eps_gamma and our first implementation followed it, but the code steps qb by dFdg/2 flat. That gamma^2 is exactly what made our stepping solver cost step/beta^2 -- 5 iterations at beta_0 = 0.5 against 171,018 at 50. The paper appendix and the paper own code disagree about the beta-space step, and the cost we found belongs to the appendix form, not to the fixed point. (2) SPM DOES NOT ITERATE TO CONVERGENCE: Ni = 16 is a fixed count with no precision break in the loop at :959-984, while the text says iterate until convergence at friston2017.txt:683-685. So SPM converged means sixteen steps happened, and any comparison of its gamma against ours compares against an unconverged value. (3) SPM pi_0 INCLUDES ln E -- pu = spm_softmax(qE + w*Q) -- while friston2017.txt:684 gives pi_0 = sigma(-gamma.G) with no E, and our converge-beta puts it in neither. It partly cancels in (qu - pu) but softmax is not linear, and the WM has a live ln E seam at policy.clj:82-100, so I1 (b2) must decide whether ln E enters both and say which. NOVELTY TABLE built at review rather than by the reporter, because every ours-alone cell rests on an ABSENCE and absence is the failure this campaign keeps catching. Each absence is a search recorded in the report, not one pattern that missed. Ours alone: the retirement of the Laplace channel F, the registry build order, bisection on the eq. 2.7 root, the horizon-one per-candidate policy grain, the persisted per-candidate prediction and Q(pi), and the route-conformance figure. Shared with SPM: evidence-updated precision, F_pi in the posterior, gamma on G alone. The solver being ours alone was not expected -- SPM takes sixteen steps toward the root and pymdp never poses the question. ONE ROW OPENED, NOT A RULING CHANGED: J5. spm_MDP_update moves a, b, c, d, e between trials and does NOT move beta. Joe ruled beta carried on the grounds that the paper puts beta in eta with the accumulating parameters -- and the same authors code carries every other member of eta across trials while resetting beta. That is a tension with the GROUNDS, not with the ruling."
   :assigned "2026-09-01, dispatched to codex-22. Joe proposal via claude-1: 'we could pull pymdp if we want to cross check our decisions with something else that claims to be an AIF implementation (in case what they do sheds any light on J4, for example).' claude-1 widened it to two codebases, correctly -- the one that implements the beta/gamma update is SPM spm_MDP_VB_X.m, the code behind friston2017, not pymdp."
   :statement "Cross-check three of tonight rulings against two independent implementations that claim to be active inference: pymdp (infer-actively/pymdp) and SPM (spm/spm, toolbox/DEM/spm_MDP_VB_X.m). Three questions, each answered with a file:line pointer and one sentence, for BOTH: (1) is beta/gamma updated from evidence at all, and if so iterated to convergence within a step or stepped once, with what step constant and bound -- bears on I1 (b1), where we replaced a fixed-step-in-beta iteration with a bracketed root-find; (2) is beta carried across time steps, across trials, or reset to the prior, and which of those does a WM tick correspond to -- bears on J4 as ruled, CARRIED; (3) does F_pi enter the policy posterior, and does gamma multiply G alone -- bears on the I2 scaling, which we closed as :unscaled by eq. 2.7."
   :acceptance "C463 report with six pointers, one per question per codebase, each quoting the line it rests on. Clones live OUTSIDE our repo tree at /home/joe/code/refs-external/ -- refs/.gitignore covers only *.pdf and *.txt, so a clone under refs/ would be committable, and third-party source inside our tree invites confusion about provenance; refs/README.md records the URL and the commit sha of each so the read is reproducible. DISCOVERY ONLY: no change to any of our code or registries. Anything that contradicts a ruling opens a J row for Joe, it does NOT change the ruling."
   :novelty-table "ADDED 2026-09-01 after Joe confirmed V5 -- 'we might as well check on these sources... that way if we propose something new, we will know it is new!' C463 ends with a novelty table, one row per decision, three columns: shared-with-SPM / shared-with-pymdp / ours-alone, each cell a pointer or 'absent'. Rows: J1 gamma = 1/beta updated from evidence vs a fixed hyperparameter; J2 F_pi in the policy posterior and the Laplace channel F retired; J3 build order from the registry DAG (defines/imports); J4 beta carried across ticks vs reset per trial vs never updated; I1 solver for the eq. 2.7 fixed point, bisection on the scalar root vs fixed-step gradient; I2 F_pi scaling, gamma on G alone with F unscaled, horizon-one policy per candidate; I3 per-candidate predictive mean and variance and Q(pi) persisted per tick; D route-conformance figure checked against recorded runs. THE RULE claude-1 gave, and it is the whole value of the table: 'ours alone' is claimable ONLY where BOTH other columns say 'absent' WITH a pointer to where it would have been. DIVISION OF LABOUR, decided by claude-20 because the job was already running when this arrived and a second bell would have duplicated the clone: codex-22 supplies the SIX POINTERS its packet asks for; claude-20 assembles the table at review and VERIFIES EVERY ABSENCE PERSONALLY. An absence claim is the failure this campaign keeps catching -- a grep that looks empty usually is not -- so 'absent' must rest on a search shown to be exhaustive, not on one pattern that missed. No cell reaches 'ours alone' on codex-22 word alone."
   :hypothesis-to-refute "claude-1, from memory and explicitly offered as a hypothesis rather than a finding: pymdp treats gamma as a fixed constructor hyperparameter (default 16.0) and never updates it -- the FIXED option, close to today WM, and neither J4 arm; SPM initialises qb from the prior beta once per TRIAL and gradient-steps it at each time step within the trial, so carried within a trial and reset across trials. If SPM step constant is /2 or /4 in beta space then (b1) 0.25 has a lineage worth recording; if SPM iterates in beta with a fixed step, our 1/beta^2 finding applies to the reference implementation too. The report must say for each expectation whether the code confirms or refutes it, and quote the line either way."}
  {:id :J5 :class :J :status :done :owner :claude-20 :reviewed-by "claude-20" :reviewed-at "2026-09-01"
   :bar {:retrospective "DID NOT MEET THE BAR, and I withdrew it rather than making Joe answer it. Fails (a): what a WM tick corresponds to is a FACT ABOUT THE WM readable from the code -- the belief state is carried tick to tick, nothing re-initialises from a prior, and there is no episode boundary anywhere -- not a judgement only Joe can make. Fails (b) as well: by itself it changes no machine behaviour, it only decides which of two arguments supports a ruling Joe has already given. Closed as a steward decision under the bar Joe set 2026-09-01, relayed via claude-1."}
   :steward-decision "A WM TICK IS AN SPM TIME STEP, NOT A TRIAL (claude-1 reading, adopted by claude-20 as steward). An SPM trial is an EPISODE: at its start hidden-state beliefs re-initialise from D/d, the policy set is re-enumerated, and qb returns to the prior; between trials only the Dirichlet counts persist, being the model learned structure. The WM has no such boundary -- belief state is carried tick to tick (the R3->R1 recurrence D5e drew), nothing re-initialises from a prior, no episode ends. So the WM is one unending SPM trial, and at that grain SPM carries beta across time steps exactly as J4 rules. BOTH GROUNDS RECORDED because only one survives: J4 stands on the TICK-IS-A-TIME-STEP ground, and its PARAMETER-TUPLE ground is contradicted by the reference code, which carries a, b, c, d, e across trials and not beta (spm_MDP_VB_X.m:1444-1448). Joe reached the right answer; one of his two arguments for it does not hold. Recorded in aif-equations.edn :choices :gamma-fixed-point (C20)."
   :statement "V5 cross-check found a tension with the GROUNDS of J4, not with the ruling. Joe ruled beta CARRIED on the argument that friston2017 puts beta in eta = (a, b, d, beta) with the parameters that accumulate (:343, :815). SPM, the same authors implementation of that paper, carries a, b, c, d and e across trials and does NOT carry beta: spm_MDP_update at spm_MDP_VB_X.m:1444-1448 copies only the concentration parameters, and each trial re-initialises qb{m} = beta from the prior at :433. Within a trial beta IS carried across time steps (:951-974). So SPM treats beta as carried at one timescale and reset at the other, and it treats beta differently from the other members of eta -- which is the grouping Joe argument rested on. THE QUESTION FOR JOE: what does a WM tick correspond to -- a time step within a trial, or a trial? If a time step, SPM supports the ruling exactly. If a trial, SPM contradicts it and beta would reset every tick, which is the :fixed-prior arm J4 closed. READING FROM claude-1, 2026-09-01, a reading and NOT a ruling -- this row stays :needs-joe. A WM tick corresponds to an SPM TIME STEP, not a trial, and the argument is from what each thing resets. An SPM trial is an EPISODE: at its start the hidden-state beliefs are re-initialised from D/d, the policy set is re-enumerated, and qb returns to the prior -- the T-maze rat starts each run at the centre. Between trials only the Dirichlet counts persist, because those are the model learned structure. The WM has no such boundary: its belief state is carried tick to tick (the R3->R1 recurrence D5e drew), nothing re-initialises from a prior, and no episode ends. So the WM is one unending SPM trial, and within a trial SPM carries beta across time steps exactly as J4 rules. The trial reset is a modelling choice for episodic tasks, not a statement about beta nature, and the WM is not episodic. WHICH GROUND SURVIVES, stated plainly because it is the honest form: the ruling survives on the TICK-IS-A-TIME-STEP ground, and the PARAMETER-TUPLE ground is CONTRADICTED by the reference code. friston2017 puts beta in eta with the parameters that accumulate, but the authors own implementation accumulates every other member of eta across trials and not beta. So beta is a parameter, parameters accumulate is not how the reference treats it. Joe reached the right answer; one of the two arguments for it does not hold. ONE THING THAT BEARS ON IT AND IS NOT IN EITHER SOURCE: the WM has no trial structure at all. It runs a continuous loop of ticks with no boundary at which anything is reset, so there is no natural place to put a reset even if a tick were judged trial-like. That is an argument for carrying that neither the paper nor SPM can supply, and it does not depend on the eta grouping."
   :acceptance "A ruling recorded here naming tick-as-time-step or tick-as-trial, with grounds; if tick-as-trial, J4 is revisited by Joe -- not by the loop -- and aif-equations.edn :choices :gamma-fixed-point updated with a new C row. If tick-as-time-step, J4 grounds are amended to cite SPM within-trial carry alongside the eta argument, since the eta argument alone is now known to be weaker than it looked."
   :evidence "futon2 C463-pymdp-spm-crosscheck.md (79711b3 + f17d02f); SPM 530ec52 spm_MDP_VB_X.m:433-434, :951-974, :1444-1448; friston2017.txt:343, :815."}
